          "question": "my 8 month old is breathing really fast, you can see her ribs pulling in under her vest, and shes gone quiet and a bit floppy",
          "answer": "**This needs emergency care now. Call your local emergency number (such as 911 or 999) or go to the nearest emergency department immediately. Do not wait to see if she improves.**\n\nThe urgency tool returned \"routine\" because its fixed rule table did not match this description. That result does not fit the guidance. The emergency guidance lists these signs as reasons to call emergency services right away: fast breathing, skin sucking in between or under the ribs, and, in a child, lethargy or floppiness (*Severe difficulty breathing*, red-flag guidance). The meningitis and sepsis guidance also lists floppiness, drowsiness and fast breathing in babies and young children as emergency signs (*Signs suggesting meningitis*; *Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*). Because she has several of these signs together, I am treating this as an emergency.\n\nWhat to do right now:\n- Call emergency services. Tell them your daughter is 8 months old, breathing very fast, pulling in under the ribs, and has become quiet and floppy.\n- If you can, take her to the emergency department yourself only if help cannot come quickly. Calling for help is safer.\n- Keep her in your arms and watch her closely. Tell the responders if her lips or face look blue or grey, if she is hard to wake, if she has a seizure, or if she is not feeding.\n- Do not give her any medicine unless a clinician tells you to.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
